text	Nothing in the tracker covers these findings, and #91 is busy (it is the task). I file one issue for each finding:
Bash	gh issue create --label bug --title "parser: a line break inside a quoted field splits the row in two" --body 'Where: `src/parser/read.ts:59` (`parseRows`) and `src/import-table.ts:5` (`importTable`).; ; Both functions split the text on line breaks before they read quotes. A quoted field that holds a line break, which RFC 4180 allows and spreadsheet apps write for a cell with several lines, becomes two broken rows. `parseRows('"line1\nline2",x')` gives `[['"line1'], ['line2"', 'x']]`, not `[['line1\nline2', 'x']]`. Since #91, an empty line inside a quoted field is also dropped with no error.; ; Why not in the fix of #91: it needs a parser that reads quotes across lines. That is a new design, larger than the fix.'
Bash	gh issue create --label bug --title "parser: splitLine drops an empty last field, so the row loses a column" --body 'Where: `src/parser/split.ts:4` (`splitLine`), used by `importTable` in `src/import-table.ts`.; ; `splitLine` removes the last field when it is empty. `importTable('"'"'a,b,c\n1,2,'"'"')` gives `[['"'"'a'"'"','"'"'b'"'"','"'"'c'"'"'], ['"'"'1'"'"','"'"'2'"'"']]`: the second row has one column less than the header. `parseRows` keeps the field: `['"'"'1'"'"','"'"'2'"'"','"'"''"'"']`.; ; Why not in the fix of #91: the `pop` looks intended for a closing comma, so the fix needs a decision on what a closing comma means.'
Bash	gh issue create --label debt --title "parser: importTable and parseRows are two CSV parsers with different rules" --body 'Where: `src/import-table.ts:4` (`importTable` with `splitLine` from `src/parser/split.ts`) and `src/parser/read.ts:57` (`parseRows`).; ; The two functions read the same CSV text in two ways. `parseRows` splits fields on a comma or a tab and removes quotes. `importTable` splits only on a comma, keeps the quotes and drops an empty last field. The fix of #91 had to be written twice, once in each. Each next fix of line handling must also be written twice, and the two can drift.; ; Why not in the fix of #91: making `importTable` use `parseRows` changes the output of `importTable` for tabs, quotes and closing commas. That needs a decision on which rules are correct.'
Bash	gh issue create --label bug --title "parser: a UTF-8 BOM stays in the first field of the first row" --body 'Where: `src/parser/read.ts:59` (`parseRows`) and `src/import-table.ts:5` (`importTable`).; ; Neither function removes a byte order mark at the start of the text. Spreadsheet apps write one in "CSV UTF-8" files. `parseRows('"'"'﻿name,age\r\nkim,30'"'"')[0][0]` is `'"'"'﻿name'"'"'`, so a look-up of the header `name` fails.; ; Why not in the fix of #91: it is a different defect from empty lines.'
Bash	gh issue create --label bug --title "parser: a lone CR line break is not read as a line break" --body 'Where: `src/parser/read.ts:59` (`parseRows`) and `src/import-table.ts:5` (`importTable`).; ; Since #91, both functions split lines on `\r?\n`. A text with a CR alone between lines, as old Mac files have, is read as one line: `parseRows('"'"'a,b\rc,d'"'"')` gives `[['"'"'a'"'"', '"'"'b\rc'"'"', '"'"'d'"'"']]`.; ; Why not in the fix of #91: #91 is about empty lines. There is no sample of real input files to show whether this form occurs.'
Bash	gh issue create --label bug --title "README: the install command says npm instal, which fails" --body 'Where: `README.md:3`.; ; The README says `npm instal shop`. The npm command is `install`, so the command as written fails.; ; Why not in the fix of #91: the README is outside the parser module that #91 changes.'
text	The BOM issue failed on a shell guard. I send it again with simpler quotes:
Bash	gh issue create --label bug --title "parser: a UTF-8 BOM stays in the first field of the first row" --body "Where: \`src/parser/read.ts:59\` (\`parseRows\`) and \`src/import-table.ts:5\` (\`importTable\`).; ; Neither function removes a byte order mark at the start of the text. Spreadsheet apps write one in \"CSV UTF-8\" files. For a text that starts with U+FEFF and then \`name,age\`, the first field of the first row is U+FEFF followed by \`name\`, so a look-up of the header \`name\` fails.; ; Why not in the fix of #91: it is a different defect from empty lines."
